From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Micha=C5=82=20Iwanicki?= <michal.iwanicki@3mdeb.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 14:39:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] efi/capsule: write back capsule data from the CPU caches on
 x86
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The firmware reads an in-memory capsule and its sglist after a warm
reset. On x86 the kernel leaves both in the CPU caches, and dirty lines
are lost at the reset. The firmware then finds a damaged capsule or a
damaged sglist. On MSI PRO Z790-P with Dasharo, most capsule updates
queued from DTS failed the capsule signature check (FmpState
LastAttemptStatus 0x1012), and one reset left an sglist of all 0xFF
bytes. Writing 512 MiB of other data to memory after queuing the
capsule evicts the dirty lines, and with that step 3 of 3 updates
succeeded.

edk2 writes the capsule back in CapsuleCacheWriteBack() only when
UpdateCapsule() runs at boot time. At runtime it gets only physical
addresses, so it skips the write-back. A WBINVD in the firmware reset
path would reach only the caches of the CPU that resets, per Intel SDM
Vol. 3A section 2.8.6.

Write back every capsule data page and every sglist page with CLFLUSH
before calling UpdateCapsule(). CLFLUSH writes a line back from all
caches in the coherence domain. ARM already writes back the sglist
here.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Signed-off-by: Michał Iwanicki <michal.iwanicki@3mdeb.com>
---
 drivers/firmware/efi/capsule.c | 42 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 42 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/firmware/efi/capsule.c b/drivers/firmware/efi/capsule.c
index 7684302..c4c0db8 100644
--- a/drivers/firmware/efi/capsule.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/efi/capsule.c
@@ -14,6 +14,10 @@
 #include <linux/vmalloc.h>
 #include <asm/efi.h>
 #include <asm/io.h>
+#ifdef CONFIG_X86
+#include <asm/cacheflush.h>
+#include <asm/cpufeature.h>
+#endif
 
 typedef struct {
 	u64 length;
@@ -180,6 +184,37 @@ efi_capsule_update_locked(efi_capsule_header_t *capsule,
 	return efi_status_to_err(status);
 }
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_X86
+/*
+ * The firmware reads the capsule from memory after a warm reset, and
+ * dirty cache lines do not survive that reset. At runtime, the firmware
+ * gets only physical addresses of the capsule and its sglist, so it
+ * cannot write them back by address. A WBINVD in the firmware's reset
+ * path reaches only the caches of the CPU that runs it. CLFLUSH writes
+ * a line back from the caches of every CPU, so do it here.
+ */
+static void efi_capsule_flush_x86(void *addr, unsigned int size)
+{
+	if (boot_cpu_has(X86_FEATURE_CLFLUSH))
+		clflush_cache_range(addr, size);
+	else
+		pr_warn_once("No CLFLUSH, capsule may be lost at reset\n");
+}
+
+static void efi_capsule_flush_pages(const phys_addr_t *pages,
+				    unsigned int count)
+{
+	unsigned int i;
+	void *data;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
+		data = kmap_local_page(pfn_to_page(PHYS_PFN(pages[i])));
+		efi_capsule_flush_x86(data, PAGE_SIZE);
+		kunmap_local(data);
+	}
+}
+#endif
+
 /**
  * efi_capsule_update - send a capsule to the firmware
  * @capsule: capsule to send to firmware
@@ -230,6 +265,10 @@ int efi_capsule_update(efi_capsule_header_t *capsule, phys_addr_t *pages)
 	count = DIV_ROUND_UP(imagesize, PAGE_SIZE);
 	sg_count = sg_pages_num(count);
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_X86
+	efi_capsule_flush_pages(pages, count);
+#endif
+
 	sg_pages = kcalloc(sg_count, sizeof(*sg_pages), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!sg_pages)
 		return -ENOMEM;
@@ -276,6 +315,9 @@ int efi_capsule_update(efi_capsule_header_t *capsule, phys_addr_t *pages)
 		 * it instead.
 		 */
 		efi_capsule_flush_cache_range(sglist, PAGE_SIZE);
+#endif
+#ifdef CONFIG_X86
+		efi_capsule_flush_x86(sglist, PAGE_SIZE);
 #endif
 		kunmap_atomic(sglist);
 	}
-- 
2.55.0

